st two terms  and this will be equal to one minus one  over k plus two factorial  this is exactly what we wanted to prove  so we've shown that the induction  formula is true  so this formula is in fact the partial  sum up to n terms  from here we want the value of the  infinite series which will be equal to  the limit as n goes to infinity of s n  and we look at this term as n goes to  infinity this term will go to zero  therefore the limit as n goes to  infinity of s n is exactly equal to one  thus the value of the infinite series is  equal to 1.  this is the first way we can solve the  problem  now let's use a telescoping sum  as before we look at the partial sum up  to n terms now we'll do a trick  what is k divided by k plus 1 factorial  equal to  in the numerator we'll add and subtract  1.  then we will split this into two  different fractions  one fraction will be k plus one divided  by k plus one factorial and then we  subtract one divided by k plus one  factorial  the first fraction we can simplify  the k plus 1 factors will cancel  so the numerator becomes 1 and the  denominator becomes k factorial  we then subtract 1 over k plus 1  factorial  so we'll use this  formula to simplify our partial sum  sn the first term is equal to one over  two factorial  using this formula this will be equal to  one over one factorial minus one over  two factorial  we then apply this formula to the next  term  this will be equal to one over two  factorial minus one over three factorial  we continue to apply this formula to  every single term in this partial sum  now we notice some interesting  cancellations  this pair of terms will cancel out  because negative 1 over 2 factorial plus  1 over 2 factorial is exactly equal to  0.  then the following pair of terms will  cancel out and this will happen for  every single pair of terms until we get  to one before the final term and that  will also cancel out  so this pattern is reminiscent of a  telescope that retracts upon itself  for this reason this type of sum is  known as the telescoping sum  so only the very beginning and the very  end survive  so only the first term and the very last  term survive so we once again get the  same formula for the partial sum to get  the value of the infinite series we take  the limit as n goes to infinity  again this term will go to zero as n  goes to infinity so the value of the  infinite series is equal to one  and that's the answer  so now let's go to a third way to solve  this problem  we will use a power series  so we want to evaluate this infinite  series  a general term of this will be k minus 1  divided by k factorial  now this is very similar to the power  series for e to the power of x  so we want to modify this power series  because we need instead of k over k  factorial we need k minus 1 over k  factorial  so we'll divide both sides by x so we  get e to the power of x divided by x  is equal to 1 over x plus one over one  factorial plus x over two factorial and  so on  from here we'll do a clever trick  we'll differentiate both sides with  respect to x  the left-hand side will use the quotient  rule  so we get x times e to the power of x  minus e to the power of x all over x  squared and on the right hand side we'll  differentiate term by term  we get negative one over x squared plus  one over two factorial plus 2x over 3  factorial and so on  now we will evaluate this at the value  x is equal to 1.  we'll do that because we'll get exactly  s and i'll show you in a second so we  substitute x is equal to 1 on the  left-hand side we get 1 times e to the  power of 1 minus e to the 1 all over 1  squared  then we substitute x is equal to 1 on  the right-hand side  so let's simplify the left hand side  the left-hand side will become e to the  power of 1 minus e to the power of 1 all  over 1. e to the power of 1 minus e to  the power of 1 is equal to zero so the  left hand side becomes zero  on the right hand side we'll simplify  the first term is negative one over one  then we get plus one over two factorial  plus two over three factorial plus three  over four factorial and so on  and now we have an interesting thing  the first term is negative one but then  the rest of the terms are exactly the  sum s  so from here on out these terms are  exactly equal to s so we can substitute  in then we add one to both sides and we  get that s is equal to one and once  again we figure out the value of this  infinite series but this time we use the  method of a power series  a very interesting way to solve this  problem  thanks for making us one of the best  communities on youtube see you next  episode of mind your decisions where we  solve the world's problems one video at  a time  you

